A||||10||rogergarrison||Laptop Cmptrs for the Cmptr Guys||||5||
Z||000000||rogergarrison||08-30-2001||03:35 PM||||Im looking to buy a new laptop, this one is toshiba 400 hz/64 whatever. I hate working the little pointy mouse so toshiba is out. Im looking at 3 diff new ones. Sony is 15" screen, others are 13.5. all are 800-850hz/128 with DVD-ROM drives, Sony has 15gb harddrive, Others have 10gb. I just want to run stuff faster than this one, I dont have a lot of programs I use in fact I only got half of what this drive will hold. So my choices are now Sony, HP, Compaq. All around the same price and have Itell Celeron. Whats the best of these? Also they all have 'Windows Milenium' loaded. Will all my programs like for my digital camera, etc work? I have 'Windows 98' on this one. Is milenium better? In your explanations, keep it simple, im not too cmptr literate.<P>thanks a bunch<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||08-30-2001||04:12 PM||||win98SE or Win2k are good, I'd recommend staying away from WinME or XP...<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieronv||08-30-2001||04:45 PM||||The question of the lcd size is preference (how good are your eyes and what resolution are you going to be using) I personally like a larger screen. Sony's are good quality but very high priced. On a laptop 10 gigs should be fine. I have seen a lot of problems with HP's screens (very poor quality). So with the 3 choices that you have given I would go with the Compaq (best bang for the buck). The question of win ME get it off your system as fast as possible, load win 98SE back on your system and update all the drivers before adding other programs onto it. Win2k used more in a business settings and isn't very friendly for most home users but is very stable. Win XP is based on the win2k platform with support for the home users and should be stable in it's final code release but I wouldn't put it on until they have updates for it (springtime). Hope that this answers a few of your questions.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||08-30-2001||05:11 PM||||I run my old a$$ IBM Thinkpad 560.  800x600x64K, 12.1" TFT LCD and I don't find myself getting eyestrain from a screen that small.  <P>95 was on it before and ran fine.  But I run Linux on it now and it runs better.  But if you're looking at Windows, go with 98SE or 2K.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||08-30-2001||05:18 PM||||My laptop (Dell) runs just fine on Milenium. I'd say when they come loaded with it on there it'll be OK. Win2K likes lots of memory to run right. You need 256megs memory for it to be happy.<BR>Drivers for Milenium are usually on the vendors site for older applications. Not everything has a Win2k driver.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sour Lemon||08-30-2001||05:36 PM||||I personally don't like Millenium...it just crashes way too much.  Win98se and win2k are pretty good choices.  <BR>As for laptops I find that sony support is really really bad.  I bought a 505 superslim last year only to find out that it has a 90 day battery warrenty.  Sure enough 97 days later my battery would only hold a charge for 20 minutes.  Toshiba is a pretty good brand.  Compaq makes some good low-end laptops.  HP is not really known for great laptops.<BR>Have you check out some of the IBM's?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||08-30-2001||09:56 PM||||Tammy (my wife) and I bought an HP, a couple of months ago. Has Win ME loaded on it. Only glitch that I've found is that it sometimes doesn't shut down correctly. (Must be a carryover "feature" from Win 98.) <BR>In order of preference, I'd buy an HP as first choice, or maybe a Sony, although I don't know much about them. Next, Dell. Then Toshiba.<BR>Compaq or IBM would be my last choice.<BR>Compaq supposedly has horrid customer service, unless they've improved. <BR>I used to support IBM laptops. Very quirky. And slow.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||09-01-2001||10:43 AM||||Beware laptops that come with Win98 or ME if you plan to use NT or 2000. They will likely have hardware components that are not compatible (no drivers). I have an older Compaq Presario 1255 that is a great machine with the OEM Win98 install. I tried a clean install (not using the restore package) and it had a very proprietary, not publicly available video driver. My Mom is using it and it does a fair job of running QuickBooks (what she uses most) and e-mail with an AMD 366Mhz/96Mb config.<P>I have a Compaq Armada M700 that shipped with Win 2k and performs extremely well. PIII 866/512Mb and compatible with anything I have a use for (lots!). The Armada is a better platform and is not sold at the big box stores. You can find them competitively priced on eBay and other places. Big display and 5.5 hours on batteries with the multi-bay extra one installed (you have to remove the DVD to use 2 batteries).<P>Your best bet is to shop the mfr websites and compare their different lines. I would guess that Dell, IBM, et al like Compaq would have a better, more flexible platform aimed at corporate users that you won't find at Best Buy with a pile of rebate stickers. After you see what you want, check the newsgroups to find out what the users have trouble with (deja.com is good for this). And then decide if those are things you can live with.<P>Don't freak about customer service. There really isn't much anymore unless you have a retailer willing to work with you. Maybe I have just had a bad run of it or my expecations are too high. Everything from cable TV to new cars you pretty much have been kissed off once you write the check. Just try to pick one that you like and hope all goes well.||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||09-01-2001||11:45 AM||||ME has most of the same compatability issues as 2k but without the good networking and protected memory of 2k.  I'm running Win 2k on my 4 year old 350mhz 128mb ram computer and it runs a lot better than 98.  I do know of some games that don't work properly under 2k... so if you really like your games stick with 98SE.  The best part of 2k is that you can't go back to anything else after using it because it doesn't really crash.  The protected memory is awesome and if Explorer ever crashes, it just restarts in the background like nothing ever happened.  No "blue screen of death" or restarting the computer in the middle of your work.<P>As far as laptops go... IBM thinkpad are cool and the Sonys get better every year as far as I can tell.  I'd have trouble buying an HP or Compaq just because their desktops suck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Even though the Compaq laptops do look nice.  Personally, I love the little "eraser" pointers on the IBMs and Toshibas.  My Dad's Dell has both the pointer and the touch pad.  When I use it at home though, I just plug in an optical mouse and use the mouse on the couch.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-01-2001||03:44 PM||||I've sold or worked on nearly every line of lap top there is at one point or antoher. They ALL suck. (This info applies to all "name brand" PC's but especially laptops.)<P>Rule # 1. Buy the Manufacturer's Extended Warantee NOT the store's third party one. Best Buy, Comp USA, Sears, and others will all try to push the store warantee on you. DONT BUY IT! This practice is called "pushing POM" and the sales people make fat commisions for doing it. "POM" (Piece Of Mind) contracts are nearly 100% proffit for the store.<P>I.E. if you buy a compaq do directly to compaq and get the extended protection from them. If the laptop breaks out of warantee you may as well throw it out and get another. A single service module can be 600-1000$ or more. Remember, Laptops are NOT desktops. nearly every part in them is custom work. Even standard looking parts may be "tweeked" so they only work in a specific laptop or the laptop may only accept certain brands/sizes of hard drive.<P>Store warantee contracts are handled by various third parties and are ALWAYS a problem if you have to get something fixed under it. I've never seen a repair under one of these contracts that didn't have some kind of problem caused by the third party contract carier. Every one I know that handled this kind of work has terminated their agreements with cariers because they don't want to pay and take forever to pay the few dollars you get. The actual service company nearly always looses money on these deals.<P>Factory warantee can be done by any authorized outlet or the maker will "sendabox" it. With sendabox they send you a box, you put the thing in the box and hand it back to the airborn/fedex driver. It goes to the service center, gets fixed, and sent back to you.<P>Factory authorized repair centers can get most parts overnight or 2nd day. Except toshiba.... god only knows when you'll get parts from them. Some things come next day others take a week or more and you never know which it will be.<P>Also keep in mind.... screen breakage, where the panel actually cracks, IS NOT covered under most warantee or extended service plans. Toshiba offers a plan for their product called "T Screen" that covers this. It's a seperate item above the warantee plans. Some other makers also have screen policies you can buy.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||09-01-2001||05:01 PM||||Am i understanding correctly that an 800/128 will run programs faster than my current Tosh 400/64. I dont do any games, just my personal business stuff, Ebaying, web surfing. All the number stuff confuses me.<P>on the Toshiba 1555CDS, the little eraser mouse is a TPITA, I use a infared mouse at home but have to use that damn button away so I def want a touchpad. Think Im leaning toward the Sony mostly because of their reputation and 15" screen. It also burns cds which the other 2 dont. The guy down the street who runs a computer only store, says the Millenium is best right now, that It almost fixes itself if theres a problem. Something about if it screws up you can just go back to the day before it screwed up and its fixed, whatever that means. He also says anything less than Pentium IV is a waste of money? I will get the manufacturers extended warranty too.<P><BR>Oh yea, whats my Toshiba worth used, anyone want to make an offer? Or would it be worth it just to keep it for backup?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
